\begin_layout Title
Individual Decision Making/Preferences
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
October 15, 2013
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Individual decision making has always been at the heart of economic study.
 In fact, economics is a social science primarily dedicated to the analysis
 of how economic agents respond to incentives and make decisions in a world
 with scarce resources.
 Many early models utilized weak and genral assumptions about agent rationality
 that enabled economists to generate strong market level results.
 Recently, however, models have become increasingly sophisticated as the
 issues of risk, uncertainty, and strategic decision making are taken into
 account, which leads to stronger assumptions concerning agent rationality.
 Consequently, it is more now more likely individual agents violate models
 due to the additional, stricter assumptions.
 Thus, experimental studies of individual decision making seem to focus
 on how and why models are violated, along with the important implications
 that arise.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Kahneman and Tversky (1979) offered a critique of expected utility theory
 as a descriptive model of decision making under risk.
 The authors analyzed a series of responses to hypothetical choice problems,
 and they found numerous inconsistencies with the three axioms of expected
 utility theory.
 In particular, people underweight outcomes that are merely probably as
 opposed to outcomes that happen with certainty.
 Also, people disregard the common aspects of different lotteries.
 To account for the several discrepancies their surveys revealed, the authors
 present an alternative model, known as prospect theory.
 In this model, value is assigned to gains and losses rather than to final
 assets.
 Moreover, probabilities are replaced by decision weights.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Kahneman, Knetsch, and Thaler (1990) looked to understand why measures of
 willingness to accept greatly exceed measures of willingness to pay, contrary
 to theoretical predictions.
 They also analyzed the predictions of the Coase theorem.
 In order to do this, they compared a series of market experiments for mugs
 against a controlled market for 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

induced-value
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 tokens.
 According to the theory, both markets should have produced identical results.
 However, in the markets for mugs an evident 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

endowment effect
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 persists even in market settings with opportunities to learn.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Andersen, Harrison, Lau, and Rustrom (2008) designed experiments in order
 to evalue the relationship between risk and time preferences.
 Many models using dynamic expected utility functions assume agents are
 risk-neutral, and then discount rates are estimated.
 Yet, joint elicitation of risk and time preferences results in significantly
 lower discount rates than separate elicitation of discount rates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The experiments conducted by Brown, Chua, Camerer (2009) were used to assess
 the two explanations for undersaving in lifecycle models: bounded rationality,
 and a preference for immediacy.
 It was found that subjects saved too little initially, but learned to save
 optimally after repeated lifecycles.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

It is evident from the above descriptions about the assigned papers that
 many of them have the same goals in mind: discern how and why models are
 not always applicable in the real world.
 These experiments tend to focus on the different assumptions that individuals
 may violate.
 Fewer experiments are constructed in order to explain the way people formulate
 their preferences, or how they make decisions.
 What motivates people to respond to positive and/or negative incentives?
 These are some of the areas that are ripe with opportunities for additional
 research and experimentation.
\end_layout

